panel per stroke showing completed strokes in black, the current stroke highlighted with a red number and directional arrow at its starting point, and remaining strokes ghosted in light gray. Use this skill whenever the user asks how to write a Japanese character, requests stroke order, asks \"in what order do I draw X\", wants to learn or memorize a kanji, asks to decompose a character into strokes, says \"show me the strokes for X\", mentions 書き順 / kakijun, or shows a stroke-order chart and asks for one for a different character. Trigger even when the user only provides a single character and the intent is implicit from the language-learning context.\n- kanji-stroke-order-html: Generate a complete standalone HTML study sheet for a Japanese kanji — stroke-order SVG inlined directly into the HTML body, plus a structured template with ON reading, KUN reading, stroke count, radical decomposition, mnemonic, 3-4 examples, and a sentence using the kanji. Produces a single self-contained .html file that opens correctly in any browser AND in Claude Code's preview (no external file references). Use this skill whenever Matthieu learns a new kanji during his Japanese study sessions and needs the rich study sheet saved into Memory/Japanese/KanjiProgress/. For raw SVG without HTML wrapper, use the sibling skill `kanji-stroke-order-svg` instead.\n- kanji-stroke-order-svg\n- karpathy-guidelines: Behavioral guidelines to reduce common LLM coding mistakes. Use when writing, reviewing, or refactoring code to avoid overcomplication, make surgical changes, surface assumptions, and define verifiable success criteria.\n- masterclass-builder: Use this skill to design rigorous, deep masterclasses and multi-module curricula (roughly 90 minutes per module, extensible to 15-20h) that build durable understanding and autonomous capability, not passive \"AI slop.\" Trigger this whenever the user wants to create a masterclass, a course, a deep curriculum, a structured learning program, progressive exercises on a topic, a study plan, or wants to genuinely LEARN a domain (not just get a one-shot answer), even if they do not use the word \"course.\" Covers learner profiling, 80/20 syllabus design, mechanism-level explanation (why before how), scaffolded progressive exercises, active recall, decision-forcing outputs, multi-agent adversarially verified research, fact verification, and an anti-slop self-audit.\n- matthieu-context\n- memory-context\n- mocap-take-from-youtube\n- plan-then-execute: Crée un plan court, attend la validation explicite de l’utilisateur, puis exécute et vérifie la tâche. Utiliser quand l’utilisateur demande de planifier avant d’agir ou veut valider une opération non triviale.\n- post-vignette: Prépare un post réseaux sociaux (Instagram) multilingue EN/FR/JP + applique le vignettage doux validé aux photos du post. Utiliser dès que Matthieu prépare un post Instagram/social sur le Japon ou ailleurs, demande de corriger/adapter un texte de post bilingue ou trilingue, demande des hashtags, ou demande un vignettage/assombrissement des bords sur des photos (mots déclencheurs : \"post\", \"insta\", \"vignettage\", \"vignette\", \"effet sur les bords\", \"hashtags\", \"adapte la traduction\"). Même si une seule des deux moitiés est demandée (texte seul ou photos seules), utiliser ce skill.\n- product-research\n- progress: Écrire et relire l’unique journal chronologique global de Matthieu dans ~/.claude/PROGRESS.md, quel que soit le projet et que l’agent soit Claude Code ou Codex. Utiliser automatiquement à chaque tâche, décision, mesure, échec, blocage, reprise ou demande « où en est-on ». Ne jamais créer de PROGRESS.md dans un projet.\n- recap-simple\n- recit-francais: Écrire de la narration littéraire en français avec le système complet des temps du passé (passé simple, imparfait, plus-que-parfait, passé antérieur, subjonctif imparfait, conditionnel comme futur du passé) et une prose de facture française, non calquée sur l'anglais. Déclencher 